==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|arreinen}}, {{m|enm|areinen}}, {{m|enm|araynen}}, {{m|enm|arrainen}}, {{m|enm|arainen}}, {{m|enm|arenen||to call (a person) to account, to call upon one to answer for himself on a criminal charge}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=arreinen|id=MED2325}}</ref> from {{der|en|xno|areiner}}, {{m|xno|arener}}, from {{der|en|fro|araisnier}}, {{m|fro|areisnier}}, {{m|fro|aresnier||to speak to, address; accuse (in a law court)}} (whence modern {{cog|fr|arraisonner||to verify cargo, to arraign}}), from {{der|en|VL.|*arratiōnāre}}, from {{der|en|la|adratiōnāre}}, from {{af|la|ad|*ratiōnāre|t1=to|t2=to reason, talk reasonably, talk}}, from {{m|la|ratiō||reason, reasoning, discourse}}, from {{m|la|rat-}}, past-participle stem of {{m|la|rērī||to reckon, calculate}}.<ref name=OED>{{R:OED2|volume=I|page=644|pos=''v.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1}}</ref><ref name=Etymonline>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref> {{etydate|late 14th century}}. {{doublet|en|areason}}.

About the -g- within the word, ''{{w|Etymonline}}'' and the ''Merriam-Webster Online Dictionary'' both agrees that it is present by [[hypercorrection]] and appears since the 16th century.<ref name=Etymonline/><ref>{{R:Merriam-Webster}}</ref> The ''[[w:Webster's Dictionary#Webster's New International Dictionary 1909|Webster’s Revised Unabridged Dictionary]]'' (1913) and the ''Concise Oxford Dictionary of English Etymology'' (1986) however, provides two etymological links each, which are {{der|en|fro|aragnier}}<ref>{{R:Webster 1913}}</ref> and {{m|fro|araigner}}<ref>{{cite-book|en|editor=T. F. Hoad|isbn=0-19-863120-0|title=The Concise Oxford Dictionary of English Etymology|year=1986|year_published=1992|publisher=The Softback Preview|location=w:Hong Kong|publisher2=w:Oxford University Press|newversion=by arrangement with|nodate2=1|entry=arraign|pages=22–23}}</ref>. The ''{{w|Oxford English Dictionary}}'' (1885, 1989) did not support either of these hypotheses, but did attribute {{der|en|fro|arraigner}}, {{m|fro|arainer}} to an unrelated obsolete {{l|en|sense|id=any particular meaning of a word}} and {{l|en|etymon|id=original or earlier form in language}}.<ref name=OED/>

